  "_key_note": "`position` is part of the IDENTITY, not a payload field, and the entry was missing the COLUMN for it entirely. `2026_09_14_200030_create_tf_updates_tables.php:38-46` ships `Schema::create('tf_updates', … $table->integer('seq'); $table->smallInteger('position'); … $table->primary(['account_id', 'seq', 'position']);`, and the migration's own docblock states why in one line — \"seq is the delivering container's seq … and position is the update's index within that container (0 for a lone update)\". All twenty `tf_updates_*` children ship the same three-column primary. `MirrorTableResolver::deriveKeyColumns()` takes an explicit `key` outright, and `MirrorKeySpec::childColumns()` copies it verbatim onto every child, so a `key` of `[\"seq\"]` made the resolver mint `(account_id, seq)` for the parent AND for all twenty children — the resolver was contradicting twenty-one shipped `primary()` declarations. Declaring the ordinal in the key (as sibling `tf_update_differences` already does against the same shape of DDL) is what reconciles them. The value is supplied by `UpdateIngestor`, which writes `position` explicitly rather than reading it from the payload.",
